#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151834 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "3399cd95-be77-59d2-89af-eb2afb5b401d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21795" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n1. $y'' = x\\cos(x), \\quad y(2)=7$\n2. $y'' + 3y = x^2+1, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y(5)=0$\n3. $2y'' - y' = e^{2x}, \\quad y(-1)=1, \\quad y(1)=3$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y$, then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$).\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "8a785a30-197d-52a4-b97c-2a104a6c4b33" :question/math-academy-id "e-21796" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0, \\qquad 1\\leq x \\leq 3$\n\n$y(1) = 0, \\qquad y(3) = -5$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\quad$ The governing differential equation is $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ For this BVP, the $AAAAA$ boundary conditions are $AAAAA$\n\n$\\quad$ The boundary value problem is $AAAAA$." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem,\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b]$, we have the following boundary condition classifications.\n\n- Dirichlet:\n$y(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Neumann:\n$y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Robin (mixed):\n$\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i$, and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\gamma_1 = \\gamma_2 = 0.)\n$$\n\nOtherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f \\equiv 0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n$y''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0$.\nSince the right-hand side $f(x) \\equiv 0$, the equation is $\\textrm{homogeneous}$.\n- The boundary conditions are\n$y(1) = 0, \\qquad y(3) = -5$.\nThese are $\\textrm{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}$.\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8c898fc-6802-5296-9866-c844f9ed0349" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Boundary Conditions"}] :question/id #uuid "f12de7ea-6a7c-5f95-80f4-ee729f0c12f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21800" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y''(x) + 2y(x) = x, \\qquad y(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1, \\qquad 0\\leq x \\leq 2$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?\n\n1. Robin\n2. Dirichlet\n3. Neumann\n4. Periodic" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem,\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b]$, we have the following boundary condition classifications.\n\n- Dirichlet:\n$y(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Neumann:\n$y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Robin (mixed):\n$\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Periodic:\n$y(a) = y(b), \\qquad y'(a) = y'(b)$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i$, and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x = a = 0$ and $x = b = 2$.\n\n$$\ny(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions."}]}
